    - target: {fileID: 1055789876787810633, guid: 7ffcca573e1296d47a9191342db0701b,
        type: 3}
      propertyPath: title
      value: 3D SSAO = Screen space ambient occlusion
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 1055789876787810633, guid: 7ffcca573e1296d47a9191342db0701b,
        type: 3}
      propertyPath: description
      value: 'In the real world, ambient light has a hard time entering and exiting
        tight spaces. This can be seen as shadows on corners. This effect is called
        Ambient Occlusion

        Screen Space Ambient Occlusion (SSAO) calculates
        this effect at runtime, usually by looking at the depth buffer.


        Usually,
        a number points are sampled within a hemisphere oriented along the surface
        normal around the current pixel. Depending on how many points are behind
        the depth buffer value, the ambient occlusion is stronger and less ambient
        light will reach that point.


        SSAO can be calculated int the fragment
        shader or as a post processing effect. Doing so in the fragment shader has
        the following properties:

        + The Ambient Occlusion only affects Ambient
        light. Just making the spots darker in post processing without knowing if
        there is directional or emmissive light for example is incorrect.

        +
        Can be applied to specific materials. Toon shaders might not look good with
        SSAO for example.

        - Blurring is not really practical.

        -
        Depth pre-pass required.

'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 1055789876787810633, guid: 7ffcca573e1296d47a9191342db0701b,
        type: 3}
      propertyPath: linkedMaterial
